#
# =====================================================================
# TERRARIUM GUEST HARDENING
#
# Appended to libkrunfw's config by the Makefile ($(KERNEL_GZ)) and resolved
# with `olddefconfig`. Appended *last*, so every value here overrides the one
# set above it in libkrunfw's own config.
#
# What this is for: the hop from the unprivileged workload (uid 1000) to guest
# root. It is not what isolates the guest from the host — that is the VM
# boundary, and guest root is grantable by design (`terra run --root`). It
# raises the cost of a workload breaking out of its user and onto the virtio
# surface as root.
#
# Note the ceiling that follows from that: everything here is depth, not a
# boundary, so where depth costs a workload this sandbox exists to run, the
# workload wins. Unprivileged user namespaces are the case where it does — see
# the note at the bottom, which is the one deliberate hole in the file.
#
# All of it is config-time, baked into vmlinux: libkrun composes the kernel
# cmdline itself, so nothing here may depend on a boot parameter.
#
# It lives in this repo rather than as an edit to the submodule's tracked
# config so that a fresh clone builds the same kernel this repo is tested
# against — an uncommitted change in a submodule working tree is not a
# mechanism, and CI checks the pin out clean.
# =====================================================================

# --- KASLR: was OFF. Randomize kernel image + memory layout. ---
CONFIG_RANDOMIZE_BASE=y
CONFIG_RANDOMIZE_MEMORY=y

# --- Heap/stack randomization on syscall entry (default was left off). ---
CONFIG_RANDOMIZE_KSTACK_OFFSET_DEFAULT=y

# --- Memory initialization: kill uninitialized-memory infoleaks + UAF reads. ---
# INIT_STACK_ALL_ZERO uses the compiler (no gcc plugin needed).
# CONFIG_INIT_STACK_NONE is not set
CONFIG_INIT_STACK_ALL_ZERO=y
CONFIG_INIT_ON_ALLOC_DEFAULT_ON=y
CONFIG_INIT_ON_FREE_DEFAULT_ON=y
CONFIG_ZERO_CALL_USED_REGS=y

# --- Slab/page allocator hardening (harder heap grooming / cross-cache spray). ---
# CONFIG_SLAB_MERGE_DEFAULT is not set
CONFIG_SLAB_FREELIST_RANDOM=y
CONFIG_RANDOM_KMALLOC_CACHES=y
CONFIG_SHUFFLE_PAGE_ALLOCATOR=y

# --- Bounds-check every copy_to/from_user against slab/stack object size. ---
CONFIG_HARDENED_USERCOPY=y

# --- CET Indirect Branch Tracking: hardware ROP/JOP defense on indirect calls. ---
CONFIG_X86_KERNEL_IBT=y

# --- The arm64 counterparts to the x86-only symbols in this file. ---
# One fragment feeds both architectures, and `olddefconfig` drops a symbol whose
# arch dependency is unmet *without saying so* — so the x86 lines above and these
# are each inert on the other host, and neither is an error. Listing both is what
# keeps an aarch64 guest from silently booting with the ROP/JOP defenses missing
# because they happened to be spelled X86_.
#   BTI_KERNEL      — branch target identification; the direct IBT counterpart.
#   PTR_AUTH_KERNEL — return-address signing, which x86 has no equivalent of.
# There is no counterpart to RANDOMIZE_MEMORY (RANDOMIZE_BASE above covers arm64
# KASLR) or to the vsyscall lines (arm64 has no vsyscall page to drop).
CONFIG_ARM64_BTI_KERNEL=y
CONFIG_ARM64_PTR_AUTH_KERNEL=y

# --- Cheap stack-overflow canary check on schedule(). ---
CONFIG_SCHED_STACK_END_CHECK=y

# --- Unprivileged BPF is a recurring privesc surface; disable by default. ---
# (BPF_JIT already off above.) Equivalent to kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled=1.
CONFIG_BPF_UNPRIV_DEFAULT_OFF=y

# --- Drop the legacy vsyscall page (a fixed-address ROP/JOP target). ---
# CONFIG_LEGACY_VSYSCALL_XONLY is not set
CONFIG_LEGACY_VSYSCALL_NONE=y

# --- Kernel lockdown LSM, forced to confidentiality at build time. ---
# Baked default (no cmdline needed): blocks kernel-memory reads, raw MSR/IO,
# and other integrity/confidentiality-breaking paths from even root-in-guest.
CONFIG_SECURITY_LOCKDOWN_LSM=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_LOCKDOWN_LSM_EARLY=y
# CONFIG_LOCK_DOWN_KERNEL_FORCE_NONE is not set
# CONFIG_LOCK_DOWN_KERNEL_FORCE_INTEGRITY is not set
CONFIG_LOCK_DOWN_KERNEL_FORCE_CONFIDENTIALITY=y

# --- Yama: a process may ptrace only its own descendants. ---
# The LSM is inert until the sysctl is set — its default scope is 0, which
# restricts nothing — so agent-init writes ptrace_scope=1 on every boot
# (crates/terra-agent/src/init.rs, `restrict_ptrace`). Building it in without
# that write is the worst of the three states: the config reads as though the
# protection is there. Both halves are needed; do not drop either.
CONFIG_SECURITY_YAMA=y

# --- Restrict kernel-pointer/dmesg leaks to unprivileged users (KASLR defense). ---
CONFIG_SECURITY_DMESG_RESTRICT=y

# --- Remove kernel-memory disclosure & raw-hardware interfaces from the guest. ---
# CONFIG_PROC_KCORE is not set
# CONFIG_DEVMEM is not set
# CONFIG_DEVPORT is not set

# --- Drop binfmt_misc: interpreter registration is needless privesc surface. ---
# CONFIG_BINFMT_MISC is not set

# --- Unprivileged user namespaces: deliberately LEFT ENABLED. ---
# CONFIG_USER_NS=y comes from libkrunfw's config and nothing caps
# user.max_user_namespaces, so uid 1000 can unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER) and hold
# CAP_SYS_ADMIN inside the namespace it made. That is the largest single surface
# for the uid-1000 -> guest-root hop the rest of this file is about, and it is
# open on purpose.
#
# It is open because rootless podman *is* this mechanism, not a consumer of it:
# any control that stops uid 1000 entering a user namespace stops podman, and
# running a container build inside the box is a workload terra exists for. There
# is no setting where one works and the other does not.
#
# And it is affordable because guest root is not the boundary — the VM is (see
# the README's security model). A workload that wins this race gets root in its
# own box, which `terra run --root` hands out for the asking anyway.
#
# Considered and rejected, so it is not re-litigated from scratch:
#
#   - Capping user.max_user_namespaces per box from a recipe field. Works, and
#     buys real depth for every box that runs no containers — but it is a config
#     surface, a plan field and a failure mode to explain, for a hop the VM
#     already contains. Revisit if boxes ever share a kernel.
#   - Dropping NF_TABLES/NF_CONNTRACK/BRIDGE/VETH. Tempting: guest-side netfilter
#     does nothing for terra (egress is enforced host-side in the gateway, which
#     never consults it), so it is close to pure attack surface, and rootless
#     podman's default networking is pasta/slirp4netns — userspace stacks over
#     TUN that need none of it. Kept anyway, because it would break
#     `podman network create` and any image that runs iptables, and the win is
#     depth on a hop that is already conceded. This is the first thing to take if
#     that ever changes.
#
# What a namespace does NOT reach, so it is not a way around the mapped
# ownership on a share: virtiofs is not FS_USERNS_MOUNT, and a nested namespace
# holds no CAP_SYS_ADMIN over the namespace owning that superblock — neither
# mounting one nor `X-mount.idmap` on the mounted one is available from inside.
#
# Unprivileged BPF is disabled above and this is not, which looks inconsistent
# and is not: no sandbox workload legitimately loads BPF, and one legitimately
# runs a container.
# =====================================================================
# end TERRARIUM GUEST HARDENING
# =====================================================================

# =====================================================================
# UNUSED FILESYSTEMS
#
# Not hardening in the sense above, and it is worth being honest about that:
# this is size. libkrunfw's config is general-purpose — krunvm, podman machine
# and anyone else embedding it — while terra mounts an exactly known set, all of
# it decided at build time by this repo:
#
#   ext4       the boot volume, the root image and every data volume
#   virtiofs   every host share. Keeps FUSE_DAX and FS_DAX with it: terra asks
#              for a 512 MiB DAX window (krun_add_virtiofs3 in libkrun_ext.rs)
#   overlay    never mounted by terra, but rootless podman *inside* the box is a
#              workload this sandbox exists for, so it stays. Do not take it.
#   proc, sysfs, devtmpfs, devpts, tmpfs, cgroup2
#
# Nothing else can turn up. A box has no network filesystems (libkrunfw ships
# none to begin with), no removable media, and every block device it ever sees
# is an image baked by the Makefile. So the parsers below are bytes in a kernel
# that ships inside the binary and is unpacked on every host that runs one.
#
# The attack-surface argument is secondary and deliberately not overstated: none
# of these carry FS_USERNS_MOUNT, so a workload holding CAP_SYS_ADMIN in its own
# user namespace cannot mount a crafted image with them regardless.
#
# This is the thing to revisit if a box ever needs one of them — an XFS-formatted
# data volume, or an erofs container image mounted in-guest.
# CONFIG_BTRFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_XFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_EROFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_FAT_FS is not set
# CONFIG_VFAT_FS is not set
# FAT was the only consumer of NLS, but NLS is a top-level option with no
# dependencies — olddefconfig keeps it (and its codepage tables) at =y with
# nothing left to use it, so it has to be taken by name.
# CONFIG_NLS is not set
#
# Left alone on purpose, though neither is mounted by terra: HUGETLBFS, because
# a workload can legitimately ask for it and it is cheap, and the fsnotify family
# (INOTIFY/FANOTIFY), because file watchers are what a dev box does all day.
# =====================================================================
# end UNUSED FILESYSTEMS
# =====================================================================
